schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: "Capital Gains (Crypto, US)"
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    Under US tax treatment, crypto is property; capital gains/losses are
    calculated from cost basis (what you paid, including fees), and taxed
    differently depending on holding period: short-term (held ≤1 year, taxed
    as ordinary income) vs long-term (held >1 year, taxed at 0/15/20%).
  source_traditions:
    - US Internal Revenue Service property-taxation framework, as applied to crypto.
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Presented as the foundational concept the rest of the article's
    taxable/non-taxable event tables and DeFi/NFT tables build on.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - cost basis
    - short-term vs long-term holding
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Taxable events (selling, trading crypto-for-crypto, spending crypto,
      mining/staking income, airdrops, hard-fork receipts) vs non-taxable
      events (buying with USD, wallet-to-wallet transfers, charitable
      donations, gifts under $15,000).
    - >-
      Ordinary income treatment (mining/staking/airdrops) vs capital
      gains/loss treatment (sales/trades) — two different tax categories for
      different crypto activities.
  tensions:
    - >-
      The article notes NFT "collectible" status (28% rate) is contested
      because NFTs are not physical, unlike the traditional collectibles the
      28% rate was designed for.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    This is explicitly US-specific guidance; presenting "crypto tax rules"
    without a jurisdiction qualifier risks readers outside the US applying
    US-specific thresholds (e.g. the $15,000 gift-tax figure) and forms
    (Form 8949, 1099-DA) to a jurisdiction where they don't apply.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  high_risk: true
  public_use: not-public-yet
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/tax-implications.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
